sec.p:
The reflectance response we obtained from various environments has wavelength-dependent characteristics. We can use various indices to highlight these signatures. One of the most commonly used indices is the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI). The index is calculated using bands in the NIR and visible red using
\begin{equation*}
N D V I=\frac{N I R_{800}-R E D_{670}}{N I R_{800}+R E D_{670}}
\tag{1}\end{equation*}where subscript values represent reflectance at a specific wavelength (nm) [18]. As the name suggests, NDVI ranges from -1 to 1, and measures how vegetated an area is, with higher values indicating greater vegetation.

Another useful index is the Normalized Difference Water Index
\begin{equation*}
N D W I=\frac{N I R_{860}-S W I R_{1240}}{N I R_{860}+S W I R_{1240}}
\tag{2}\end{equation*}for which high values are intended to indicate the presence of water content in vegetation [19]. However, wavelengths of Green and NIR bands are used to detect water bodies [20], [21]. The subscripts here denote the approximate corresponding center wavelengths used in this study.
\begin{equation*}
N D W I=\frac{\text{GREEN}_{\sim 560}-N I R \sim 850}{\text{GREEN}_{\sim 560}+N I R_{\sim 850}}
\tag{3}\end{equation*}

We can also calculate the Normalized Difference Snow Index [20], using Green and SWIR bands that could range from 545 nm and 565 nm and 1628 nm and 1652 nm, respectively.
\begin{equation*}
N D S I=\frac{\text{GREEN}_{\sim 550}-S W I R_{\sim 1640}}{G R E E N_{\sim 550}+S W I R_{\sim 1640}}
\tag{4}\end{equation*}
